# Caching headers for Cloudflare's asset pipeline. Workers static assets reads
# this file as deploy configuration and keeps it out of the uploaded assets, the
# same way Pages did — wrangler 4.130.0 lists `_headers` among the paths it
# ignores when building the manifest, and sends its contents alongside it.
#
# These are NOT documentation or a fallback, whatever the old comment here
# said. This file, not worker/index.js, is what sets these headers in
# production: `run_worker_first` is unset, so a request matching an existing
# asset is answered by the router and never reaches the Worker script. The
# Worker runs only on a miss, which is where it turns the SPA fallback into a
# real 404. Measured on the Inbox migration — `wrangler dev` reports
# `Parsed 1 valid header rule` and serves the header on a real asset.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/_expo/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# EVERY response, and the reason this rule has to exist at all: Cloudflare Pages
# added these two headers to every response it served, automatically, with
# nothing in this repository asking for them. Workers Static Assets does not, so
# without this rule the move to a Worker would drop both from clarity.surf —
# silently, because no build, deploy or test here reads a response header.
#
# Measured on a Pages hostname whose project has no `_headers` rule for them
# either, which is what shows they come from the platform rather than from any
# project:
#
#   oxy-auth.pages.dev  referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
#                       x-content-type-options: nosniff
#
# `nosniff` is the one that matters: without it a browser may disregard a
# declared Content-Type and sniff the bytes instead, which turns any response
# carrying attacker-influenced content into a possible script.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
